<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Blantyre</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Blantyre is the main commercial city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shire-highlands">Shire Highlands</a> of the south. It was founded in 1876 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a> mission and named after the Scottish birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a>. The mission and the trading posts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-lakes-company">African Lakes Company</a> drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> into the region and led to the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/blantyre.jpg" alt="Blantyre" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SMuyila · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blantyre_City.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Blantyre lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shire-highlands">Shire Highlands</a>, at the geographical centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>'s Southern Region, and covers an area of 228 km2. The city stands in hilly country at elevations from about 780 to 1,612 metres. Mount Soche, Ndirande mountain, Chiradzulu mountain and Michiru Mountain surround it, and rivers and streams rise in these hills and radiate from the city. The rainy season runs from November to April, and the dry season from May to October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The mission was founded in 1876 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a>. The site was chosen by the British missionary Henry Henderson, who was joined there on 23 October 1876 by Dr T. T. Macklin and others. Macklin took over the leadership and began building. The first ordained minister, Rev. Duff MacDonald, arrived in 1878. The first missionaries faced local opposition, and three of them were recalled. From 1881 to 1898 the mission was run by David Clement Scott, who built the Church of St Michael and All Angels between 1888 and 1891 with a team of local workmen who had no knowledge of European building techniques. Scott also compiled an Encyclopaedic Dictionary of the Mang'anja Language, later edited by his successor Alexander Hetherwick.</p><p>A trading company grew up beside the mission. The Livingstonia Central Africa Trading Company, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> in 1877 and renamed the African Lakes Corporation in 1878, moved in 1881 to Mandala, across the River Mudi. Its Mandala House survives as a National Monument and is the oldest building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>. Blantyre became a British consular station in 1883 and a municipality by 1895, Malawi's oldest. Its commerce began with the colonial trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> its people included Africans from neighbouring districts and South Asian traders.</p><p>Until 1905 the town's growth was confined to three areas within 2 km of each other: the mission, Mandala, and the government and commercial triangle. Limbe began to develop in 1906, after the Shire Highlands Railways Company set up its headquarters and workshops there and the Imperial Tobacco Group built a packaging and grading factory nearby. Indian wholesale and retail shops multiplied, and by 1910 Limbe had surpassed Blantyre. The two towns were merged into one city in 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Blantyre is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>'s centre of finance and commerce and its second largest city, with 800,264 people at the 2018 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is the capital of the Southern Region and of Blantyre District. The Malawi Stock Exchange, opened in November 1996, stands on Victoria Avenue. The city holds eight National Monuments, mostly historic buildings, and the Michiru Mountain Conservation Area lies among its hills.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Blantyre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scottish missions at <b>Blantyre</b> and Livingstonia and the African Lakes Company drew Britain into the Lake Malawi region.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-central-africa-protectorate">British Central Africa Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
